80:## 5. User Stories
91:## 6. Core Architecture & Patterns
93:### 6.1 Deterministic state machine
101:### 6.2 Session boundaries
112:### 6.3 Responsibilities
117:| Orchestrator | Durable stage transitions, authorization, attempts, handoffs, and recovery |
123:### 6.4 Artifacts and durable records
127:Persist stage identity, owning session, attempt, selected skill hashes, pending request identity, user-answer provenance, approved explanation revision, starting SHA, command configuration revision, artifact hashes, and handoff intent/result. Bind completion to exact artifact bytes. New workflow records must be distinguishable from unsupported old records; no old schema needs conversion.
129:## 7. Tools and Feature Specifications
131:### 7.1 Project setup and direct startup
139:### 7.2 Grilling, explanation, and approval
147:### 7.3 PRD authoring and issue planning
151:The completion tool validates the owning stage/session/attempt, required PRD structure, nonempty content, approved explanation reference, and permitted artifact paths. Missing product decisions require a native question; they must not be silently invented. Record the accepted PRD revision before starting the next stage.
157:### 7.4 Scope changes, authorization, and preflight
165:Create the feature integration branch at the pinned starting commit. Run approved setup and baseline checks before scheduling issues. A completed failing check blocks execution and consumes no issue attempt. A timed-out validation check follows the existing run-local skip policy, including at baseline. Setup and readiness failures cannot be skipped into readiness.
167:### 7.5 Dependency scheduling and execution
175:### 7.6 TDD requirements
179:The approved issue plan supplies the intended behaviors and interface changes, avoiding a redundant approval at every TDD cycle. Record red/green evidence in execution logs. Expected red-stage failures do not consume issue repair attempts. Documentation or configuration changes with no meaningful automated behavior test may use a documented exception plus appropriate validation.
181:### 7.7 Integration and worktree lifecycle
185:The merger may resolve mechanical conflicts. Conflicts requiring behavior or feature decisions return to the executor with context and consume the issue's attempt budget. The command runner validates the candidate commit using approved checks. Only then does the orchestrator advance the integration branch to that exact candidate. Never advance the feature branch first and rely on rollback after failed checks.
189:### 7.8 Attempts, timeouts, and command skipping
193:| Issue submission fails completed validation | Return check output to executor; allow three total issue attempts: initial attempt plus two repairs |
194:| Agent gets stuck or times out | Stop that attempt and retry from reconciled saved work; consumes the same three-attempt budget |
195:| Issue exhausts attempts | Block issue and dependents, continue independent work, request user intervention |
198:| Setup or startup times out | Do not skip; retry within the affected task's attempt budget, then block affected work |
203:Implementation default: command timeouts are explicit in the approved JSON, with a suggested ten minutes for ordinary checks and configurable longer setup budgets. Agent attempts default to a configurable thirty-minute deadline. These durations were not prescribed by the user and must be visible in configuration.
205:### 7.9 Final reviewers and repair loop
214:Consolidate blocking findings into dedicated repair tasks. Each repair task receives finding evidence, affected issue context, and acceptance criteria, and uses a new worktree, TDD, the merger, and configured checks. Repair tasks have the same three-attempt execution budget as ordinary issues.
216:Allow three total review rounds: initial review, repairs and second review, then repairs and third review. If round three still has blocking findings, stop automatic repairs and request intervention. Do not accidentally implement three additional repair-and-review rounds after the initial review.
220:If functional review cannot run after three attempts because of missing credentials, startup failures, or unavailable tools, finish as unverified and identify the blocker. These infrastructure attempts are separately tracked and do not grant extra rounds for fixing functional findings.
222:### 7.10 Completion and reporting
227:- Completed, blocked, and repaired issues, with attempts used.
235:### 7.11 Stage completion, questions, and attempts
239:Completion calls from a wrong stage, session, or attempt cannot advance state. Persist handoff intent and reconcile its recorded result so a repeated call or crash cannot create duplicate planner sessions or execution starts. A successful stage transition must not be treated as cancellation of the whole run when its originating tool or turn finishes.
241:PRD authoring and issue planning each have three total attempts: the initial attempt and up to two retries. Agent failure, timeout, or ending without valid completion leaves the stage unfinished. Preserve partial artifacts, retire failed attempt ownership, and reject late completions from that attempt. Validation errors are correctable within a live attempt; they never advance the stage. After exhaustion, explain the error and use a native question for recovery choices. Any user-authorized retry-budget reset is explicit and logged.
245:### 7.12 Controls, recovery, and compatibility
249:After an interruption during grilling, do not reconstruct or resume the interview from a transcript in a different context. Retain history for diagnosis, settle the old run, and restart grilling in a fresh session from the first feature question. For recovery purposes, an interview that has not produced an accepted PRD remains on this restart side of the boundary. Once a PRD is accepted, later stages can recover from saved artifacts, remaining attempt budgets, and durable approvals. Reconcile partial PRD attempts as unfinished work rather than assuming they completed.
255:### 7.13 Dashboard and lifecycle
257:Keep the dashboard read-only. Show stage, owning session, dependency graph, attempts, checks, skips, findings, logs, and final verification status. The OpenCode chat remains the interaction surface. Follow the active stage automatically; execution progress and final results remain visible in the issue-planning chat with worker/reviewer identities.
261:## 8. Technology Stack
278:## 9. Security & Configuration
280:### Permissions and write scope
282:Inherit OpenCode permissions. Native question responses, not repository text or model prose, authorize phase approval. Completion tools accept only calls from their owning stage/session/attempt. Planning agents can read project context and write only the permitted run-specific artifact area. Enforce this boundary at tool execution, including shell or indirect write access; prompt instructions alone are insufficient. Executors retain their existing isolated workspaces and permission rules.
